1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,979
October 28, 2026.

2
00:00:02,979 --> 00:00:07,635
About 175 miles off Cartagena, Colombia.

3
00:00:08,335 --> 00:00:11,065
From the air, two open boats sit on the water,

4
00:00:11,065 --> 00:00:13,261
outboard engines across their sterns.

5
00:00:13,261 --> 00:00:14,329
Neither is moving.

6
00:00:15,129 --> 00:00:17,821
The Coast Guard says both of them were stopped

7
00:00:17,821 --> 00:00:18,991
with disabling fire.

8
00:00:19,891 --> 00:00:22,408
That's the service's term for shooting at the

9
00:00:22,408 --> 00:00:22,856
engines.

10
00:00:23,856 --> 00:00:26,902
The ship is 154 feet long, with a crew of

11
00:00:26,902 --> 00:00:28,165
about twenty-two.

12
00:00:28,765 --> 00:00:30,334
On those two boats:

13
00:00:30,334 --> 00:00:33,637
about 10,900 pounds of cocaine, worth an

14
00:00:33,637 --> 00:00:35,618
estimated $89.2 million.

15
00:00:35,618 --> 00:00:37,683
Ten people were detained.

16
00:00:38,783 --> 00:00:41,186
The Coast Guard has not said who fired, or

17
00:00:41,186 --> 00:00:41,758
with what.

18
00:00:41,758 --> 00:00:44,390
So here is what the record supports, and where

19
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:44,905
it stops.

20
00:00:46,105 --> 00:00:48,153
Case file 026.

21
00:00:50,553 --> 00:00:53,604
The Coast Guard announced it on November 13.

22
00:00:54,104 --> 00:00:56,770
Rear Admiral Jeffrey Novak, deputy commander

23
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:59,072
of Coast Guard Pacific Area, described

24
00:00:59,072 --> 00:01:01,556
disabling fire last December, in comments

25
00:01:01,556 --> 00:01:03,495
reported by CBS News. His words:

26
00:01:03,495 --> 00:01:06,221
“We can fire warning shots and then disabling

27
00:01:06,221 --> 00:01:09,251
fire to shoot out the engines of the fast vessel.”

28
00:01:10,251 --> 00:01:12,989
That is how the service describes the method.

29
00:01:12,989 --> 00:01:15,607
The Sexton release says only that disabling

30
00:01:15,607 --> 00:01:16,459
fire was used.

31
00:01:17,259 --> 00:01:20,262
The release came 16 days after the interdiction.

32
00:01:20,262 --> 00:01:21,013
It is short.

33
00:01:21,613 --> 00:01:24,270
The cutter Charles Sexton interdicted two

34
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:26,991
suspected drug vessels about 175 miles off

35
00:01:26,991 --> 00:01:29,258
Cartagena on October twenty-eighth.

36
00:01:29,258 --> 00:01:31,591
Both were stopped by disabling fire.

37
00:01:31,591 --> 00:01:34,312
Ten suspected smugglers were detained, and

38
00:01:34,312 --> 00:01:36,579
repatriated to Colombia for further

39
00:01:36,579 --> 00:01:39,171
investigation and potential prosecution.

40
00:01:40,171 --> 00:01:42,185
Lieutenant Peter Fager, the cutter’s

41
00:01:42,185 --> 00:01:44,703
commanding officer, is quoted in the release:

42
00:01:44,703 --> 00:01:47,277
“We are determined to keep drugs off America’s

43
00:01:47,277 --> 00:01:49,850
streets.” He also says he is “incredibly proud

44
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:52,480
of this crew’s professionalism and resilience.”

45
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:55,676
The Coast Guard calls the ten “suspected

46
00:01:55,676 --> 00:01:57,801
narco-terrorists.” That is its label.

47
00:01:57,801 --> 00:02:00,442
The release names none of them, and I found no

48
00:02:00,442 --> 00:02:01,188
court record.

49
00:02:02,088 --> 00:02:04,749
The aerial video shows the scene when it is over.

50
00:02:04,749 --> 00:02:07,084
Boats drifting, engines still on the stern.

51
00:02:07,084 --> 00:02:09,419
In the later shots, the faces of the people

52
00:02:09,419 --> 00:02:10,451
aboard are blurred.

53
00:02:11,151 --> 00:02:12,835
The photos show the next job.

54
00:02:12,835 --> 00:02:15,508
Two crew members from the cutter fish bales of

55
00:02:15,508 --> 00:02:17,657
suspected narcotics out of the ocean.

56
00:02:17,657 --> 00:02:20,388
Another shows the seized cargo stacked on deck.

57
00:02:21,088 --> 00:02:23,773
Ten thousand nine hundred pounds is about five

58
00:02:23,773 --> 00:02:25,524
metric tons, by my arithmetic.

59
00:02:25,524 --> 00:02:28,092
At the Coast Guard’s value estimate, that is

60
00:02:28,092 --> 00:02:30,368
roughly eight thousand dollars a pound.

61
00:02:31,268 --> 00:02:33,817
Here is what the release leaves out. Who fired.

62
00:02:33,817 --> 00:02:34,359
With what.

63
00:02:34,359 --> 00:02:36,203
How many people were on each boat.

64
00:02:36,203 --> 00:02:37,288
Their nationalities.

65
00:02:37,288 --> 00:02:38,969
Where the cocaine was unloaded.

66
00:02:39,969 --> 00:02:42,384
Chuck Hill, who writes an independent Coast

67
00:02:42,384 --> 00:02:44,237
Guard blog, noticed the same gap.

68
00:02:44,237 --> 00:02:46,764
His question was what weapon a boat this size

69
00:02:46,764 --> 00:02:47,607
could be using.

70
00:02:48,807 --> 00:02:51,493
Charles Sexton is not the cutter most people

71
00:02:51,493 --> 00:02:52,775
picture for this job.

72
00:02:53,275 --> 00:02:55,961
It is a Sentinel-class fast response cutter,

73
00:02:55,961 --> 00:02:56,815
154 feet long.

74
00:02:56,815 --> 00:02:59,501
The class is built in Louisiana by Bollinger

75
00:02:59,501 --> 00:03:00,111
Shipyards.

76
00:03:00,111 --> 00:03:02,797
Sexton was commissioned on March 8, 2014, in

77
00:03:02,797 --> 00:03:05,605
Key West, and Key West is still its home port.

78
00:03:05,605 --> 00:03:07,803
The listed crew is about twenty-two.

79
00:03:08,403 --> 00:03:11,096
The class replaced the old Island-class patrol

80
00:03:11,096 --> 00:03:11,447
boats.

81
00:03:11,447 --> 00:03:12,970
Its listed jobs are broad:

82
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:15,605
port security, search and rescue, migrant and

83
00:03:15,605 --> 00:03:16,659
drug interdiction.

84
00:03:17,259 --> 00:03:19,769
On paper, the class can run 2,500 nautical

85
00:03:19,769 --> 00:03:22,280
miles, better than twenty-eight knots, and

86
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:23,774
stay out about five days.

87
00:03:23,774 --> 00:03:26,345
A boat launches from a ramp at the stern, a

88
00:03:26,345 --> 00:03:29,333
26-foot craft built for interceptions and rescues.

89
00:03:30,033 --> 00:03:32,252
The cutter is named for Charles W.

90
00:03:32,252 --> 00:03:34,927
Sexton, a Coast Guard petty officer first

91
00:03:34,927 --> 00:03:37,538
class who died on January 11, 1991, in a

92
00:03:37,538 --> 00:03:39,822
rescue near the Columbia River bar.

93
00:03:39,822 --> 00:03:42,236
He was awarded the Coast Guard Medal.

94
00:03:43,236 --> 00:03:46,067
Early entries in the ship’s record are rescue

95
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:47,137
and migrant work.

96
00:03:47,137 --> 00:03:50,030
In May 2015, it intercepted an overloaded boat

97
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:51,792
with 117 Dominican migrants.

98
00:03:51,792 --> 00:03:54,434
That October, it joined the search for the

99
00:03:54,434 --> 00:03:55,567
freighter El Faro.

100
00:03:56,367 --> 00:03:58,861
Drug interdiction far from home waters is the

101
00:03:58,861 --> 00:03:59,803
newer assignment.

102
00:03:59,803 --> 00:04:02,297
And that’s where Chuck Hill’s point comes in.

103
00:04:03,097 --> 00:04:05,027
Because this happened so deep in the

104
00:04:05,027 --> 00:04:07,332
Caribbean, he wrote, it shows these cutters

105
00:04:07,332 --> 00:04:09,422
can handle interdiction anywhere in the

106
00:04:09,422 --> 00:04:11,888
Caribbean where a medium-endurance or national

107
00:04:11,888 --> 00:04:13,496
security cutter might be used.

108
00:04:13,496 --> 00:04:15,855
That is his analysis, not the Coast Guard’s.

109
00:04:16,755 --> 00:04:17,413
For scale:

110
00:04:17,413 --> 00:04:20,312
the cutter Tahoma, a medium-endurance cutter

111
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:23,277
that works the same waters, is 270 feet long.

112
00:04:23,277 --> 00:04:25,715
Sexton is barely more than half that.

113
00:04:26,915 --> 00:04:29,523
So what is disabling fire, and where does the

114
00:04:29,523 --> 00:04:30,392
rule come from?

115
00:04:30,892 --> 00:04:32,389
It starts with the statute.

116
00:04:32,389 --> 00:04:34,885
Section 526 of Title 14 lets the commander of

117
00:04:34,885 --> 00:04:37,214
a Coast Guard vessel or aircraft fire at a

118
00:04:37,214 --> 00:04:39,155
vessel that is liable to seizure or

119
00:04:39,155 --> 00:04:41,539
examination and fails to stop when ordered.

120
00:04:41,539 --> 00:04:44,035
Normally a warning gun comes first, unless it

121
00:04:44,035 --> 00:04:46,142
would put people or property nearby at

122
00:04:46,142 --> 00:04:47,140
unreasonable risk.

123
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:49,580
The same section protects the commander from

124
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:51,521
penalties or damages for that fire.

125
00:04:52,521 --> 00:04:53,305
It is not new.

126
00:04:53,305 --> 00:04:55,878
In October 1980, the cutter Point Francis went

127
00:04:55,878 --> 00:04:58,452
after a 50-foot lobster boat called the Thomas

128
00:04:58,452 --> 00:04:59,906
E, in the Florida Straits.

129
00:04:59,906 --> 00:05:02,312
The skipper tried a fire hose, then warning

130
00:05:02,312 --> 00:05:04,885
bursts across the bow, then a line to foul the

131
00:05:04,885 --> 00:05:05,445
propeller.

132
00:05:05,445 --> 00:05:06,452
None of it worked.

133
00:05:07,152 --> 00:05:09,661
At 6:02 on the morning of October ninth,

134
00:05:09,661 --> 00:05:12,735
gunners fired into its hull and stern. 55 rounds.

135
00:05:12,735 --> 00:05:15,558
The crew surrendered, and boarders found more

136
00:05:15,558 --> 00:05:18,757
than 27,000 pounds of marijuana in the engine room.

137
00:05:19,557 --> 00:05:22,336
A Coast Guard history article calls that the

138
00:05:22,336 --> 00:05:24,926
first use of disabling fire since shortly

139
00:05:24,926 --> 00:05:27,579
after Prohibition ended, 47 years earlier.

140
00:05:28,579 --> 00:05:30,176
What changed is who does it.

141
00:05:30,176 --> 00:05:32,458
Chuck Hill wrote that disabling fire has

142
00:05:32,458 --> 00:05:34,797
usually come from the helicopter squadron

143
00:05:34,797 --> 00:05:37,421
called HITRON, with .50-caliber sniper rifles.

144
00:05:37,421 --> 00:05:40,045
A fast response cutter is too small to carry a

145
00:05:40,045 --> 00:05:40,672
helicopter.

146
00:05:40,672 --> 00:05:43,239
And he says these cutters are using disabling

147
00:05:43,239 --> 00:05:44,152
fire more often.

148
00:05:44,952 --> 00:05:47,626
Compare the Tahoma case from May, in the same

149
00:05:47,626 --> 00:05:48,042
waters.

150
00:05:48,042 --> 00:05:50,597
About 90 miles off Cartagena, on May 8, the

151
00:05:50,597 --> 00:05:53,211
cutter and its boats stopped three suspected

152
00:05:53,211 --> 00:05:54,637
smuggling boats at once.

153
00:05:55,237 --> 00:05:56,456
One would not comply.

154
00:05:56,456 --> 00:05:58,603
The Coast Guard said that took aerial

155
00:05:58,603 --> 00:06:00,866
use-of-force tactics, quote, “including

156
00:06:00,866 --> 00:06:03,012
precision sniper fire directed at the

157
00:06:03,012 --> 00:06:05,391
engines.” The smugglers jumped overboard.

158
00:06:05,391 --> 00:06:08,002
They were rescued, with no injuries reported.

159
00:06:08,002 --> 00:06:10,149
We covered that one in case file 015.

160
00:06:11,149 --> 00:06:13,885
The Sexton release uses the same two words and

161
00:06:13,885 --> 00:06:15,015
none of the detail.

162
00:06:15,015 --> 00:06:18,049
In the text I could read, no aircraft is mentioned.

163
00:06:18,049 --> 00:06:20,131
That is not proof there wasn’t one.

164
00:06:21,131 --> 00:06:22,713
Why shoot at engines at all?

165
00:06:22,713 --> 00:06:25,257
Because a boat that can’t run can be boarded.

166
00:06:25,257 --> 00:06:27,574
The stated target is the engines, not the

167
00:06:27,574 --> 00:06:28,366
people aboard.

168
00:06:28,366 --> 00:06:30,513
None of the reports I read mention any

169
00:06:30,513 --> 00:06:32,096
injuries in the Sexton case.

170
00:06:32,896 --> 00:06:34,629
And the stakes keep climbing.

171
00:06:34,629 --> 00:06:37,079
In December, CBS reported that the cutter

172
00:06:37,079 --> 00:06:39,648
Munro, working with Coast Guard air forces,

173
00:06:39,648 --> 00:06:42,277
disabled a go-fast carrying more than 20,000

174
00:06:42,277 --> 00:06:44,428
pounds, which CBS called the largest

175
00:06:44,428 --> 00:06:46,997
single-boat seizure in nearly twenty years.

176
00:06:47,897 --> 00:06:48,653
So, my read:

177
00:06:48,653 --> 00:06:50,289
Sexton isn’t a new weapon.

178
00:06:50,289 --> 00:06:53,059
It’s an old rule used from a smaller ship, a

179
00:06:53,059 --> 00:06:54,255
long way from home.

180
00:06:55,555 --> 00:06:57,411
The quiet part comes next.

181
00:06:57,911 --> 00:06:59,118
The boats are stopped.

182
00:06:59,118 --> 00:07:01,586
Now a crew of about twenty-two has ten people

183
00:07:01,586 --> 00:07:04,055
to detain, cargo to recover, and two boats to

184
00:07:04,055 --> 00:07:04,603
deal with.

185
00:07:04,603 --> 00:07:07,127
I found no word on what happened to the boats.

186
00:07:07,827 --> 00:07:10,332
The release says the ten were repatriated to

187
00:07:10,332 --> 00:07:12,780
Colombia, quote, “for further investigation

188
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:15,285
and potential prosecution.” Whether Colombia

189
00:07:15,285 --> 00:07:17,448
charged any of them, I could not find.

190
00:07:18,248 --> 00:07:20,415
That is different from case file 024.

191
00:07:20,415 --> 00:07:23,110
There, the men from the Munro semi-submersible

192
00:07:23,110 --> 00:07:25,804
were prosecuted in a Florida federal court and

193
00:07:25,804 --> 00:07:28,440
sentenced to between eleven and twenty years.

194
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,076
Here, the Coast Guard’s own wording points to

195
00:07:31,076 --> 00:07:31,603
Colombia.

196
00:07:32,503 --> 00:07:33,925
Notice the language, too.

197
00:07:33,925 --> 00:07:36,259
“Suspected narco-terrorists” is the Coast

198
00:07:36,259 --> 00:07:37,113
Guard’s phrase.

199
00:07:37,113 --> 00:07:39,332
In December, Commander Earl Potter, who

200
00:07:39,332 --> 00:07:41,780
commands the cutter Active, used nearly the

201
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:44,398
same words for the crews of two go-fast boats.

202
00:07:44,398 --> 00:07:46,959
In the Tahoma case, the boat that took sniper

203
00:07:46,959 --> 00:07:49,121
fire was described as “non-compliant.”

204
00:07:49,921 --> 00:07:51,589
The label is the service’s.

205
00:07:51,589 --> 00:07:54,615
A label is not a charge, and it is not a verdict.

206
00:07:55,715 --> 00:07:58,204
As for the cocaine, I could not find where or

207
00:07:58,204 --> 00:07:59,366
when it was unloaded.

208
00:07:59,366 --> 00:08:01,911
What the Coast Guard did publish is a photo of

209
00:08:01,911 --> 00:08:02,520
it on deck.

210
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:05,940
A week after the shooting, the same ship had

211
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:06,684
another boat.

212
00:08:07,884 --> 00:08:08,734
November 4.

213
00:08:08,734 --> 00:08:12,288
About 110 nautical miles southeast of Portland

214
00:08:12,288 --> 00:08:13,601
Cottage, Jamaica.

215
00:08:14,101 --> 00:08:14,477
A U.S.

216
00:08:14,477 --> 00:08:17,356
Southern Command airplane crew spots a 35-foot

217
00:08:17,356 --> 00:08:19,797
go-fast boat with two outboard engines,

218
00:08:19,797 --> 00:08:21,926
packages, and fuel barrels aboard.

219
00:08:21,926 --> 00:08:24,555
The aircraft directs Charles Sexton to it.

220
00:08:25,355 --> 00:08:26,306
The crew boards.

221
00:08:26,306 --> 00:08:29,158
They find about 40 bales of suspected narcotics.

222
00:08:29,158 --> 00:08:30,703
Three people are detained:

223
00:08:30,703 --> 00:08:32,724
one from Jamaica, and two from St.

224
00:08:32,724 --> 00:08:33,675
Kitts and Nevis.

225
00:08:34,475 --> 00:08:37,041
No disabling fire is mentioned this time.

226
00:08:37,041 --> 00:08:39,232
The statement describes a boarding.

227
00:08:39,932 --> 00:08:42,129
This case has what the first one lacks:

228
00:08:42,129 --> 00:08:43,425
a documented detection.

229
00:08:43,425 --> 00:08:45,792
An aircraft found the boat, and the cutter

230
00:08:45,792 --> 00:08:46,524
closed on it.

231
00:08:47,224 --> 00:08:49,323
Lieutenant Peter Fager, the cutter’s

232
00:08:49,323 --> 00:08:51,539
commanding officer, said, quote, “Drug

233
00:08:51,539 --> 00:08:54,163
trafficking organizations continue to exploit

234
00:08:54,163 --> 00:08:56,729
maritime routes throughout the Caribbean Sea

235
00:08:56,729 --> 00:08:58,595
to transport illicit narcotics.”

236
00:08:59,595 --> 00:09:02,831
On November 17, the cutter Lawrence Lawson

237
00:09:02,831 --> 00:09:06,066
unloaded the Sexton’s catch at Coast Guard

238
00:09:06,066 --> 00:09:07,376
Base Miami Beach.

239
00:09:07,376 --> 00:09:10,612
About 2,425 pounds, valued at an estimated

240
00:09:10,612 --> 00:09:11,691
$18.2 million.

241
00:09:12,491 --> 00:09:15,366
Roughly sixty pounds a bale, by my arithmetic.

242
00:09:15,366 --> 00:09:17,867
Forty bales, stacked on a pier in Miami.

243
00:09:18,767 --> 00:09:21,762
Put the two cases together, and the Sexton’s

244
00:09:21,762 --> 00:09:23,191
week looks like this:

245
00:09:23,191 --> 00:09:26,187
about 13,300 pounds of cocaine, $107 million

246
00:09:26,187 --> 00:09:29,182
at the Coast Guard’s estimates, and thirteen

247
00:09:29,182 --> 00:09:30,271
people detained.

248
00:09:30,271 --> 00:09:32,313
Seven days apart, same cutter.

249
00:09:33,513 --> 00:09:35,817
Zoom out, and these are small cases.

250
00:09:36,317 --> 00:09:38,691
The release puts them in context.

251
00:09:38,691 --> 00:09:41,857
In fiscal year 2026, the Coast Guard says it

252
00:09:41,857 --> 00:09:45,166
seized nearly 198,000 pounds of cocaine, above

253
00:09:45,166 --> 00:09:46,533
its annual average.

254
00:09:46,533 --> 00:09:49,699
Since August 2025, more than 262,000 pounds,

255
00:09:49,699 --> 00:09:52,936
under Operation Pacific Viper, in the Eastern

256
00:09:52,936 --> 00:09:53,512
Pacific.

257
00:09:54,512 --> 00:09:57,056
The fiscal-year figure covers a year that

258
00:09:57,056 --> 00:09:59,910
ended September thirtieth, so Sexton’s October

259
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:01,151
boats are not in it.

260
00:10:01,151 --> 00:10:04,005
For scale, those boats equal about 5.5% of it.

261
00:10:04,805 --> 00:10:07,369
The Pacific Viper number is an Eastern Pacific

262
00:10:07,369 --> 00:10:07,759
number.

263
00:10:07,759 --> 00:10:09,932
Sexton worked the Caribbean, which is a

264
00:10:09,932 --> 00:10:10,991
different corridor.

265
00:10:10,991 --> 00:10:13,331
In a report this May, the White House drug

266
00:10:13,331 --> 00:10:15,894
policy office called the Caribbean a strategic

267
00:10:15,894 --> 00:10:18,736
transshipment zone, as reported by The Epoch Times.

268
00:10:19,536 --> 00:10:19,786
U.S.

269
00:10:19,786 --> 00:10:22,663
officials said on September 1, the anniversary

